    "content": "All 19 checklist items have been tested and recorded — **17 PASS, 2 FAIL**.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe core to-do functionality is solid: task CRUD, optional description/deadline, priority assignment, both sort modes (including undated tasks sorting last), active/completed view switching with accurate counts, non-destructive completion, whitespace-title rejection, prerequisite assignment, blocked-state distinction, and both hover tooltips (dashed SVG connection lines and the blocked warning) all behaved as specified. Cycle prevention was notably robust, excluding both direct and indirect (transitive) dependents from the prerequisite options.\n\nTwo defects were found, both in the dependency subsystem:\n\n**FT-3 — FAIL: blocked tasks are entirely uneditable.** The spec requires that a blocked task still allow description and priority changes, with only title and deadline locked. In practice the edit button carries the HTML `disabled` attribute on every blocked task. A DOM scan of a blocked card confirmed the only controls are complete (disabled), edit (disabled), and delete — there is no inline or alternative path to edit anything.\n\n**CS-22 — FAIL: reopening a prerequisite creates a permanently stuck contradictory state.** This is the more serious of the two. After completing \"Prepare launch deck\" (whose prerequisite \"Buy groceries\" was complete), reopening \"Buy groceries\" was neither blocked nor did it reset the dependent. \"Prepare launch deck\" ended up *simultaneously completed and blocked* — still in the Completed view, still counted, rendered with line-through, yet displaying `Prerequisite \"Buy groceries\" incomplete`. Because blocked tasks have both their complete toggle and edit button disabled, the task becomes unrecoverable: the user cannot reopen it, cannot edit it, and can only delete it. Note that FT-3's over-aggressive disabling is what turns this invalid state into a dead end, so fixing FT-3 would also soften CS-22's impact — though CS-22 needs its own fix to cascade reopening to dependents."
  }
}
